(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["da-easy-2026-002"]={"v":1,"id":"da-easy-2026-002","title":"Advance Level Test-2 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","sourceTitle":"Advance Level Test-2 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","branch":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE","code":"DA","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI","seriesId":"da-easy-2026","num":2,"category":"advance","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI"},"source":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Data_Science_and_AI/004_Advance Level Test-2 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the value of the following expression?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/43/436737c36100a8d5650e.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/775815f36c5b93c9de4f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2c50c1196422ee583228.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bcf1e09681b6427fc176.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59dd3055cfcf5696b1fe.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74a6e238e594a509ba24.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A student took five papers in an examination, where the full marks were the same for each paper. His marks in these papers were in the proportion of 6 : 7 : 8 : 9 : 10. In all papers together, the candidate obtained 60% of the total marks. Then the number of papers in which he got more than 50% marks is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2"},{"key":"B","html":"3"},{"key":"C","html":"4"},{"key":"D","html":"5"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1d/1d528eeb164a6e990d6a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p><em>R</em>, <em>S</em> and <em>H</em> enter into a partnership and their capitals are in the proportion of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/3410e67a2b48cb45032d.png\" /> <em>R</em> withdraws half his capital at the end of 4 months. Out of total annual profit of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/ae6b9218502367c98161.png\" /> <em>R</em>’s share is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d38e0c4d792cbdaaa8f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4a/4a9adc4b7c36f4e079f2.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/668dbdf8a91ec7da4bd2.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/42/4259526ccd3a7fe25ee4.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea375a202e3afcb07691.png\" /></div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>For this question, you have to base your conclusion on the three premises (viz: 1, 2 and 3). Assuming the facts in the statements to be true, which of the following conclusions logically follows from the statements?<br />\n<strong>Statements:</strong><br />\n1. Some cakes are bikes<br />\n2. Some bikes are hills<br />\n3. All laptops are bikes<br />\n<strong>Conclusions:</strong><br />\nI. All laptops are hills<br />\nII. Some laptops are cakes<br />\nIII. Some cakes are hills<br />\nIV. Some laptops are not cakes</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only I follows"},{"key":"B","html":"Only either II or IV follows"},{"key":"C","html":"Only I or II follows"},{"key":"D","html":"Only I and IV follows"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/be9bff9429bd5d0aed43.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A messenger was returning to his base station which was in front of him to the north. When this base station was 100 m away from him, he turned to the left and moved 50 m to deliver the last message to the Peshwa’s troops. He then moved in the same direction for 40 m, turned to his<br />\nright and moved 100 m. How many meters away he is now from his base station?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"150"},{"key":"C","html":"90"},{"key":"D","html":"100"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e7b20176c1c05550a5e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A person starts typing the numbers from 1 to 1999. He press the keys total ‘n’ number of times.<br />\nThe value of <em>n</em> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6889"},{"key":"B","html":"1000"},{"key":"C","html":"2888"},{"key":"D","html":"none of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nThe number of times he pressed the keys for one digit number = 9<br />\nThe number of times he pressed the keys for two digit numbers = 90 &times; 2 = 180<br />\nThe numbers of times he pressed the keys for three digit numbers = 900 &times; 3 = 2700<br />\nThe number of times he pressed the keys for the numbers 1000 to 1999 is 1000 &times; 4 = 4000<br />\nThus, total number of times the keys are pressed = 6889</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Choose the most appropriate word from the options given below to complete the following sentence.<br />\nBased on his recent poor decisions, it was obvious that Seth lacked even a _____ of good sense.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"debasement"},{"key":"B","html":"diversion"},{"key":"C","html":"disapprobation"},{"key":"D","html":"modicum"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nModicum is a small or token amount. It effectively conveys that Seth lacked even a small amount of good sense.</p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Below is a sentence of which some parts have been jumbled up. Rearrange these parts which are labelled <em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em> and <em>S</em> to produce the correct sentence. Choose the proper sequence. It would<br />\nP : that they were quite in the dark<br />\nQ : about the policy of management<br />\nR : appear from their statement<br />\nS : in dealing with the strike<br />\nThe proper sequence should be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"S R P Q"},{"key":"B","html":"R P Q S"},{"key":"C","html":"S Q R P"},{"key":"D","html":"R S Q P"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n&ldquo;It would appear ...&rdquo; can be the only coherent beginning of the sentence. Thus the first part<br />\nwould be <em>R</em>. Since R says &ldquo;their statement&rdquo;, sentence P would follow it because it also says, &ldquo;that they ...&rdquo;<br />\nThus, option (b) is the correct answer.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MSQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If you are a brisk walker, there is no need to go to a gym. Your neighbourhood can become your gym. You don’t need fancy equipment to get a good exercise either. All you need is a welldesigned pair of shoes.<br />\nThe paragraph best supports the statement that</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"poorly designed shoes can cause major foot injuries."},{"key":"B","html":"walking outdoors provides a better workout than walking indoors."},{"key":"C","html":"a gym membership is a poor investment."},{"key":"D","html":"brisk walking is a convenient and valuable form of exercise"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D, C, B, A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nBy stating that brisk walking does not require commute to a gym, the author stresses the<br />\nconvenience of this form of exercise. The paragraph also states that brisk walking may result in a good workout.<br />\nOption (c) is not appropriate. A gym membership can be a good investment for some.<br />\nOption (b) is not given in the passage.<br />\nOption (a) seems logical but the paragraph does not mention it.<br />\nOption (d) is the most appropriate option.</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>How many triangles are there in the following figure?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aaf7f592fbedede41d37.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6"},{"key":"B","html":"10"},{"key":"C","html":"11"},{"key":"D","html":"12"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74beba64b6ff2d8dac2e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":11,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>There are 3 fair coins and 1 false coin with tails on both sides. A coin is chosen at random and tossed 4 times. If tails occurs in all 4 times, then the probability that the false coin has been chosen for tossing is _________. [Correct upto two decimal places]</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.8,0.87],"answerText":"0.84","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.84 [0.80 - 0.87]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/25/254640995de3adefccce.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If <em>A</em> is symmetric and <em>B</em> is skew-symmetric matrix, then which of the following is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>ABA<sup>T</sup></em> is skew-symmetric matrix."},{"key":"B","html":"<em>AB<sup>T</sup></em> + <em>BA<sup>T</sup></em> is symmetric matrix."},{"key":"C","html":"(<em>A</em> + <em>B</em>)(<em>A</em> – <em>B</em>) is skew-symmetric."},{"key":"D","html":"(<em>A</em> + <em>I</em>)(<em>B</em> – <em>I</em>) is skew-symmetric."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b9/b930cc73c925e0601287.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/ddb824df70e61beab2fa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following Python code:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4df0da02ce4405088050.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What will be the output when this code is executed?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Runtime Error"},{"key":"B","html":"Gate Exam Not Qualified"},{"key":"C","html":"Gate Exam Qualified"},{"key":"D","html":"Compile-time Error"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n<strong>Condition 1:</strong><br />\ns1.lower( ) == s2.lower( )<br />\ns1.lower( ) &rarr; &quot;madeeasy2026&quot;<br />\ns2.lower( ) &rarr; &quot;madeeasy2026&quot;<br />\nBoth are equal &rarr; Condition 1 is True<br />\n<strong>Condition 2:</strong><br />\ns1.isupper( ) != s2.isupper( )<br />\ns1.isupper( ) &rarr; &quot;MADEEASY2026&quot; &rarr; all letters are uppercase &rarr; True<br />\ns2.isupper( ) &rarr; &quot;MADEeasy2026&quot; &rarr; contains lowercase &rarr; False<br />\nThe if condition requires both conditions to be true: True AND True &rarr; True<br />\nSo, the program executes the first print statement.<br />\nGate Exam Qualified.</p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The manufacturer knows that the TV’s he make contain on an average 1% defective. He packs them in boxes of 100. What is the probability the box picked at random will contain 2 or more faulty TV’s _________? [<strong>Note:</strong> Use Poisson’s distribution]</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/69f34f1187b258b53953.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0c7a7f76d84f0837fe35.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"0"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a713b9d6fcd2fe78761d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The expansion of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e8017bbacb52420581c8.png\" /> is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/89/8915a3abac16e6b47ddb.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b33eb1f44f822a2fcc36.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a6/a611325b0415f7325fe6.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aaef015bd403ee5a8597.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f0f05b26919afec07033.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":16,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If the matrix <em>A</em> is defined as <em>A</em> = <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/80af2990832047fdaffe.png\" /> the sum of eigen values of 3<em>A</em><sup>3</sup> + 5<em>A</em><sup>2</sup> – 6<em>A</em> + 2<em>I </em>is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[124,124],"answerText":"124","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>124 [124 - 124]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/536d8149ac21924a8d96.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which linkage method is typically more sensitive to outliers because it relies on the maximum distance between clusters?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Average Linkage"},{"key":"B","html":"Complete Linkage"},{"key":"C","html":"Median Linkage"},{"key":"D","html":"Single Linkage"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nComplete linkage is determined by the farthest pair of points. An outlier far away from the group<br />\ncan significantly increase the &lsquo;distance&rsquo; of that cluster, delaying its merger.</p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A search space contain two different path leading to goal:<br />\n<strong>• Path A:</strong> Long path heuristic values decreasing slowly.<br />\n<strong>• Path B:</strong> Short path but heuristic values fluctuate and include occasional spikes.<br />\nGreedy Best-First Search chooses nodes with minimum <em>h</em>(<em>n</em>).<br />\nLet <em>h<sub>A</sub></em>(<em>n</em>) and <em>h<sub>B</sub></em>(<em>n</em>) denote heuristics along paths <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> respectively.<br />\nWhich condition ensures Greedy Best First Search will incorrectly follow the long path A before considering the short path B?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"max(<em>h<sub>B</sub></em>(<em>n</em>)) > min(<em>h<sub>A</sub></em>(<em>n</em>))"},{"key":"B","html":"min(<em>h<sub>B</sub></em>(<em>n</em>)) < min(<em>h<sub>A</sub></em>(<em>n</em>))"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>h<sub>B</sub></em>(<em>n</em>) is admissible and monotonic"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>h<sub>A</sub></em>(<em>n</em>) = <em>h<sub>B</sub></em>(<em>n</em>) for all nodes"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/fff52afd0ebdf23c1957.png\" /></div>"},{"n":19,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the relation R(A, B, C, D, E) with the function dependencies:<br />\nA → B, B → C, C → A, D → E and E → D<br />\nThe maximum possible super-keys of R is _________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[21,21],"answerText":"21","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>21</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f5/f5a31909121262ad1a00.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = <em>x</em><sup>1/2</sup>. The second Taylor polynomial <em>p</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>x</em>) of <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) at <em>x</em> = 1 is given. Find <em>p</em><sub>3</sub>(3). (Choose the nearest answer)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/2757fe0e6ae975605bc0.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73c77d03b9d246ec6ab8.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/62/6206cd0093e240a6fd93.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e18a073d5c1c9ad85517.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cd/cd05a29862873982a060.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/30/306fe09a7489aced9ac4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If Mean = (3 Median – Mode)A, then the value ‘<em>A</em>’ is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/8271f99d3653c3a9a84b.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/be/bef916304800d52b4d3a.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6c/6c9257e13280fbc84a21.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following matrices have eigen values as 1 and –1?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/15bec4bf5e81545612e5.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/06/06a1d2db161a7a40f4cd.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fe2f99d477d49695feea.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e49e67f112b29d0d8f9.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/cae2ee3365aa0a33269f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f791b2ef9b936ea313d3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A toy car is placed on the Cartesian plane (where <em>x</em> and <em>y</em> both are positive value). At each step it is allowed to move either one unit up or one unit right, i.e., if it is at (<em>i</em>, <em>j</em>) then it can move to either (<em>i</em> + 1, <em>j</em>) or (<em>i</em>, <em>j</em> + 1). How many distinct paths are there for the toy car to reach the point<br />\n(5, 5) starting from the initial position (0, 0)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<sup>10</sup><em>C</em><sub>5</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"2<sup>5</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"2<sup>10</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7d8bf9a8d9f25c2e0ea7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A dataset contains 120 training samples belonging to two classes.<br />\nThe entropy at a particular node is 0.94.<br />\nA split on attribute F divides the data into the following two child nodes:<br />\n• <strong>Child Node 1:</strong> 45 samples, with an entropy of 0.74.<br />\n• <strong>Child Node 2:</strong> 75 samples, with an entropy of 0.89.<br />\nLet the weighted entropy after the split be Es′ and the Information Gain obtained from the split be IG.<br />\nWhich of the following statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Weighted entropy after split is 0.83."},{"key":"B","html":"Weighted entropy after split is 1.50."},{"key":"C","html":"Information gain is 0.11."},{"key":"D","html":"Information gain is 0.58."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/ac14fec11be31ab056f3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>What is the worst case time complexity for search, insert and delete operations in a binary search tree?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ο(log <em>n</em>) for all"},{"key":"B","html":"Ο(<em>n</em>) for all"},{"key":"C","html":"Ο(log <em>n</em>) for search, and Ο(<em>n</em>) for insert and delete"},{"key":"D","html":"Ο(log <em>n</em>) for search and insert, and Ο(<em>n</em>) for delete"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e9b4b01ab55a35e5d9b4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Z-distribution is symmetric about the mean.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : A T-distribution with 1 degree of freedom is symmetric about the mean.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : A Chi-squared distribution with very large degrees of freedom (approaching infinity) is approximately symmetric about the mean.<br />\nWhich of the following statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; The Z-distribution is a standard normal distribution. It is perfectly bell-shaped and symmetric<br />\nabout its mean (0). So, S<sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; A T-distribution with 1 degree of freedom is heavily tailed and not reliably symmetric for<br />\npractical purposes. Its variance is infinite, so <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is considered incorrect. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Chi-squared distribution is right-skewed for small degrees of freedom. However, as the<br />\ndegrees of freedom increase, it approaches normality and becomes approximately symmetric.<br />\nSo, S<sub>3</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following functional dependencies of schema:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/67/67d3080422cd9f1bbfb4.png\" /></div>\n\n<p><br />\nWhich of the following is true about <em>R</em>?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"In 1NF but not in 2NF"},{"key":"B","html":"In 2NF but not in 3NF"},{"key":"C","html":"In 3NF but not in BCNF"},{"key":"D","html":"In BCNF"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nCandidate key &rarr; <em>A</em>, <em>D</em><br />\nThis relation is in 3NF but since <em>B</em> &rarr; <em>C</em> is non-prime to non-prime dependency which is not allowed in 3NF.</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The number of distinct max heap are possible with keys 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 are ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/18a604abaf67d01eee23.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":29,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a hash table with 10 slots. Collisions are resolved using linear probing. The probability that if first 3 slots are unfilled and 4th insertions leads to a collisions ________. (Upto 3 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.061,0.067],"answerText":"0.063","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.063 [0.061 - 0.067]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c8/c81065c1cba91885bfb0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":30,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A queue is implemented using two stack such that ENQUEUE and DEQUEUE operations are performed efficiently. Which one of the following statements is CORRECT (n refers to the number of items in the queue)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both operations can be performed in Ο(1) time."},{"key":"B","html":"At most one operation can be performed in Ο(1) time but the worst case time for the other operation will be Ο(<em>n</em>)."},{"key":"C","html":"The worst case time complexity for both operations will be Ω(<em>n</em>)."},{"key":"D","html":"Worst case time complexity for both operations will be Ω(log <em>n</em>)."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nENQUEUE operation will take &Omicron;(1) time.<br />\nDEQUEUE operation will take &Omicron;(<em>n</em>) time for first element after that each element will take<br />\n&Omicron;(1) time.</p>"},{"n":31,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A classifier is used to detect whether a transaction is fraud or legitimate.<br />\nFor the 8 transaction records below, the actual class y and predicted class <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f86ae963c57aa741ce6a.png\" /> are given:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d7aca87d2ee1fdf086d8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Specificity is 0.6"},{"key":"B","html":"Specificity is 1.2"},{"key":"C","html":"Accuracy is 0.625"},{"key":"D","html":"Accuracy is 1.725"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/03/03111fc8dd6f84657fa6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The context of Gibbs Sampling, which statement accurately describes the requirement for the order in which variables are updated to ensure convergence to the stationary distribution?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Must always be fixed to guarantee convergence."},{"key":"B","html":"Must be random and change every iteration."},{"key":"C","html":"Can be either fixed or random as long as all variables are updated infinitely often."},{"key":"D","html":"Does not matter because Gibbs Sampling samples all variables simultaneously."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Gibbs Sampling requires that every variable must be updated infinitely often (i.e., given enough<br />\niterations, every variable will eventually be sampled from its full conditional distribution).<br />\n&bull; The update order can follow a fixed sequence (systematic scan) or a random sequence (random<br />\nscan).<br />\n&bull; Both the fixed and random scan approaches are valid and lead to the same target stationary<br />\ndistribution.<br />\n&bull; Option (d) is incorrect because Gibbs Sampling is a sequential, single-site updating method,<br />\nnot a simultaneous one.</p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The minimum, maximum, mean, and standard deviation of the attribute monthly expenditure for<br />\na group of analysts are <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/fa/fa57aab4f8aa9bec28eb.png\" /> respectively.<br />\nWhat is the z-score normalized value for an analyst whose monthly expenditure is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d73ee489eea4210abb71.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.33"},{"key":"B","html":"0.67"},{"key":"C","html":"1.22"},{"key":"D","html":"1.89"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d11d63bc1b0ab2e531d1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In a binary classification problem using a linear SVM, the margin is defined as the distance between the two hyperplanes that are parallel to the decision boundary and pass through the closest training data points.<br />\nWhat is the relationship between the magnitude of the weight vector, <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/70ff167f38a6c5039ff9.png\" /> and the geometric margin, γ?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9c/9cc41f3bbcc645d21631.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ec/ec005fb728d5e8e3b007.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ce/ce0aed6b221f82681984.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7aa4c27366037a63783a.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fe095bf2ad2d1fb8bbc5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If the two lines of regression are perpendicular, then the correlation coefficient <em>r</em> =</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0"},{"key":"B","html":"1"},{"key":"C","html":"-1"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7a4317e125624ea50c3d.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b1164956598b40c4e5d6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em> be a symmetric 2 × 2 matrix with an eigenvalue λ<sub>1</sub> = 2 and its corresponding eigenvector</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7f/7f520aa0c086a51cc5e5.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If A is orthogonally diagonalized as <em>A</em> = <em>PDP<sup>T</sup></em>, which pair of matrices correctly represents the normalized eigenvectors matrix P and the diagonal matrix <em>D</em>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1f2a6490fefa86c3ff17.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e7f29d46c13e5b93c18c.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5bdc7d52cf0ac15a8e50.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa100846376827c6633d.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73d70dc9da6eebbd98e7.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>How many 5-digit even numbers have all 5 digits distinct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"10752"},{"key":"B","html":"13776"},{"key":"C","html":"14319"},{"key":"D","html":"3024"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/04aa62ca1a6b453246d3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A multilayer perceptron (MLP) uses a fully connected feed-forward architecture. The network consists of an input layer with 10 neurons, followed by two hidden layers containing 6 and 5 neurons respectively, and finally an output layer with 2 neurons.<br />\nEach neuron in every non-input layer has an associated bias term, and all layers are fully connected.<br />\nWhat is the total number of trainable parameters in this network?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"102"},{"key":"B","html":"107"},{"key":"C","html":"113"},{"key":"D","html":"132"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6aa28642add1260d40d1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding the Alpha–Beta pruning technique used in Minimax:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub>: Alpha–Beta pruning reduces the number of nodes expanded by eliminating branches that cannot affect the final Minimax decision, without changing the value computed by the algorithm.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In Alpha–Beta pruning, a β-cutoff occurs at a MIN node when the current value of the node becomes greater than or equal to β, because further children cannot produce a smaller value.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S<sub>2</sub></em>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/83/834ab2daebdcd9826ab3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><em>n</em> log <em>k</em> sorted sub-arrays each of size <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e76d4b975f300b925102.png\" /> are to be merged into a single sorted array. Find the time complexity of the most efficient algorithm which will give the sorted array.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/879eb2d2347f06aad403.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c9/c9d17f29cfe34868825b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e1952e08fd0439c879f.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/84/84acc539559330cb2e78.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/75/75667a4a13473ac4f8fc.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a9/a9b6e0a2549d1c1f01d4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Depth-Limited Search (DLS) with depth limit L is executed on a search tree where:<br />\n• the tree is infinite<br />\n• branching factor is infinite at some levels<br />\n• the goal is at depth ≤ <em>L</em><br />\nConsider the statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : DLS may still be incomplete even if the goal lies within the depth limit.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : DLS becomes complete only if both depth and branching factor are finite.<br />\nWhich of the above is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and S<sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Even if a goal exists at depth &le; L, an infinite branching factor at a shallower depth can cause<br />\nDLS to explore an infinite subtree before reaching the goal &rarr; incomplete. So, S<sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; DLS is complete only when<br />\n(i) the tree depth is finite<br />\n(ii) the branching factor is finite.<br />\nfor guaranteed completeness.<br />\nSo, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the function <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) given as:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b13149ba367ce273a0fa.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the below statements is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) is continuous at <em>x</em> = 2"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) has removable discontinuity at <em>x</em> = 2"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) has non-removable discontinuity at <em>x</em> = 2"},{"key":"D","html":"Discontinuity at <em>x</em> = 2 can be removed by redefining the function at <em>x</em> = 2"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/30/303adf0422c27642113c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/879e0855e06bacbf57f9.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":43,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A researcher is analyzing a multi-class dataset and decides to use Linear Discriminant Analysis<br />\n(LDA) for dimensionality reduction before classification. Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : LDA maximizes class separability by considering both between-class and within-class scatter.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : LDA can reduce the dimensionality to at most (C – 1), where C is the number of classes.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : LDA is an unsupervised dimensionality-reduction method.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>4</sub> : LDA and PCA both compute eigenvalues of the covariance matrix of the entire dataset.<br />\nWhich of the above statements are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> only"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>4</sub> only"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> only"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> only"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7a/7ac14d3a58986e17b1ca.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) = 2<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – 15<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> + 36<em>x</em> + 1 is increasing in the interval given below, choose the correct option</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/45f3a78678233aa9dc85.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e375b690f4992c3f56e5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b7/b743736e3cf93a2d1bba.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/75/751de64f144cb5f83e57.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following undirected simple unweighted graph:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e2/e2754f242acacd96ff23.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following BFS and DFS sequence are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a0/a0b2f54e344f6aaa2e0f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/37/3731dcc425c737648d3e.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e058bd2fe0072c65d812.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/540e091e70fc8a5f9818.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nRun standard BFS and DFS algorithm and that gives (d) as the correct answer.</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Suppose we have 2 bags. Bag 1 contains 3 red and 7 green balls. Bag 2 contains 4 red and 8 green balls. A person tosses a coin and if it is heads goes to bag 1 and draws a ball. If it is tails, he goes to bag 2 and draws a ball. Given that the ball drawn is red, then what is probability that it came from bag 1 ________ (upto three decimal places)?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.31,0.32],"answerText":"0.317","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.317 [0.310 - 0.320]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59dae0a92f144e9c0313.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following predicates:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/16075e87d2577a725f72.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the above conclusions can be concluded using premises <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"C<sub>1</sub> only"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>C</em><sub>2</sub> only"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>C</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>C</em><sub>2</sub> both"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>C</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>C</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c3/c3daa75dae18f26924c9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following expression is equivalent to <em>p</em> → <em>q</em>, where <em>p</em> and <em>q</em> are propositions?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b3d5bb00e5a5e2b03fd.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/36/36b78852c67164b9259b.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/99/9920f5ef8935a475a550.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/66/6684fb178b33fa1ce15e.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1e/1ee5c42f9dae71f524f6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the function y = <em>x</em><sup>2</sup> – 6<em>x</em> + 9. The maximum value of y obtained when <em>x</em> varies over the interval 2 to 5 will be at ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[5,5],"answerText":"5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>5</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8c/8c5b76dc2112fdfe3396.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Suppose the sequence 1, 0, 8, 7, 5, 3, 2, 4, 6, 9 is a pre-order binary search tree. The binary search tree uses the usual ordering on natural numbers. What is the post-order traversal sequence of the resultant tree?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"7, 5, 1, 0, 3, 2, 4, 6, 8, 9"},{"key":"B","html":"0, 2, 4, 3, 6, 5, 7, 9, 8, 1"},{"key":"C","html":"9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0"},{"key":"D","html":"0, 2, 4, 3, 1, 6, 5, 9, 8, 7"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/63342a1dacd956261ab9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following function that takes reference to head of a double linked list as parameter.<br />\nAssume that a node of doubly linked list has previous pointer as prev and next pointer as next<br />\nclass Node:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0c44629a581b84d696ff.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Assume that reference of head of the following doubly linked list is passed to above function 11 ↔ 22 ↔ 33 ↔ 44 ↔ 55 ↔ 66.<br />\nWhat should be the modified linked list after the function call?</div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"55 ↔ 44 ↔ 33 ↔ 22 ↔ 11 ↔ 66"},{"key":"B","html":"22 ↔ 11 ↔ 44 ↔ 33 ↔ 66 ↔ 55"},{"key":"C","html":"66 ↔ 55 ↔ 44 ↔ 33 ↔ 22 ↔ 11"},{"key":"D","html":"66 ↔ 55 ↔ 44 ↔ 33 ↔ 11 ↔ 22"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe function swaps prev and next for every node, reversing the links.<br />\nTraversal uses current = current.prev because pointers are swapped.<br />\nAfter the loop, temp.prev becomes the new head.<br />\nHence, the head becomes 66 and all nodes are reversed.<br />\nThe final list is 66 &harr; 55 &harr; 44 &harr; 33 &harr; 22 &harr; 11.</p>"},{"n":52,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let W be the subspace of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b154161ef491ce30a678.png\" /> consisting of all solutions to the homogeneous linear equation <em>x</em><sub>1</sub> + <em>x</em><sub>2</sub> + <em>x</em><sub>3</sub> – <em>x</em><sub>4</sub> = 0 dimension of the subspace <em>W</em> ________?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/65/6533d1500ff225084284.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/1568d98273d810cddd42.png\" /></div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"K-Fold Cross-Validation requires the dataset to be shuffled before splitting."},{"key":"B","html":"A smaller value of K (e.g., K = 3) usually increases the bias of the performance estimate."},{"key":"C","html":"K-Fold Cross-Validation reduces the effect of data splitting randomness."},{"key":"D","html":"Hold-Out method performs training K times for K folds."}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)<br />\n&bull; Shuffling is recommended, especially for ordered data, but not a requirement.<br />\nK-Fold can still run even without shuffling, though accuracy may be biased. So, option (a) is<br />\nincorrect.<br />\n&bull; When K is small, each fold has less training data, causing the performance estimate to become<br />\nmore biased.<br />\nThis is a well-known bias-variance behavior of K-Fold. So, option (b) is correct.<br />\n&bull; K-Fold averages performance across multiple train-test splits.<br />\nThis reduces dependence on any single random split, giving a more stable estimate. So, option<br />\n(c) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Hold-Out uses only one train-test split and trains the model only once.<br />\nTraining K times is done in K-Fold, not in Hold-Out. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Which of the following relation schema with given functional dependency follows BCNF?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>R</em>(A, B, C, D) with FD’s AB → C, C → D and D → A."},{"key":"B","html":"<em>R</em>(A, B, C, D) with FD’s A → B, B → C, C → D and D → A."},{"key":"C","html":"<em>R</em>(A, B, C, D, E) with FD’s AB → C, C → D, D → B and D → E."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>R</em>(A, B, C, D) with FD’s B → C and B → D."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2a0965902ee9b96d81c0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":55,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements regarding the K-Means clustering algorithm:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : The K-Means objective function is guaranteed to decrease (or remain unchanged) after each update of cluster assignments and centroid positions.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In K-Means clustering, increasing the value of K always results in a decrease in the value of the objective function.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1 </sub>and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; K-Means alternates between assigning points to their nearest centroid and recomputing centroids. Both steps reduce (or preserve) the within-cluster sum of squared distances. So,&nbsp;<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; As K increases, clusters become smaller and each point is closer to its centroid. Therefore, the<br />\nobjective function (WCSS) always decreases with larger K. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is correct.</p>"},{"n":56,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A max-heap is a heap where the value of each parent is greater than or equal to the values of its children. How many max-heaps is/are there in below figure?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/60d38f0a2fef53c68aa0.png\" /></div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2<br />\n<strong>I.</strong> Not a max heap because level-2 is not filled completely before level-3.<br />\n<strong>II.</strong> This is a max heap.<br />\n<strong>III.</strong> Not a max heap because the value of node (5) is less than its child node (8).<br />\n<strong>III.</strong> This is a max heap.</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The number of distinct simple graphs with up to three labeled nodes is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[11,11],"answerText":"11","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>11</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b7/b7458799a104db654cdc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : The time complexity of Prim’s algorithm is Ο((<em>V</em> + <em>E</em>) log <em>V</em>) when implemented using an adjacency list with a Binary Heap.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : The space complexity of Prim’s algorithm is Ο(<em>V</em><sup>2</sup>) when using an adjacency Matrix representation.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following conditions insures an <em>m</em> × <em>n</em> matrix <em>A</em> is invertible?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>m</em> = <em>n</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"There exists an <em>n</em> × <em>m</em> matrix <em>B</em> such that <em>AB</em> = <em>I<sub>m</sub></em>."},{"key":"C","html":"The row echelon form of A has the same number of pivot rows as pivot columns."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>Ax </em>= <em>b</em> has a unique solution <em>x</em> for every <em>b</em>."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a7751e0aa4af7af764b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":60,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider an undirected random graph of nine vertices. The probability that there is an edge between a pair of vertices is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/03/03ba60d9faec685f7517.png\" /> What is the expected number of unordered cycles of length three<br />\n(up to two decimal place)?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3.11,3.11],"answerText":"3.11","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3.11</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/78/78d0458d15af1d8c727a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a database that has a relation schema:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9b698b1f6f72bac8b9a2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Assume that every student is enrolled in at least one course, then which of the following query<br />\n“Retrieve the names of students who have scored grade ‘A’ in all subjects they have enrolled”?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3f/3f9e273345541b804488.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9d9a80e626ba6fd8810f.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0e03a0189502cce9ddd1.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/960438a8f95481ebe359.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIn option (b)<br />\n<strong>When Student e with all A grade,</strong> for enrollment tuples not having her roll number, LHS is false.<br />\nFor enrollment tuples having her/his roll number, LHS is true, RHS also true so the implication is<br />\ntrue for all e tuples.<br />\n<strong>When Student e with some non-S grades,</strong> for enrollment tuples not having her roll number,<br />\nLHS is false.<br />\nFor enrollment tuples having her roll number, LHS is true, but RHS is false for at least one tuple.</p>"},{"n":62,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>100 dices are thrown. How many are expected to fall either a 3 or 6 ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[33,34],"answerText":"33.3","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>33.3 [33 - 34]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/15d2a82d74f2a8fb3404.png\" /></div>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following recurrence relation:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1f078f950366e80c88f3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Find the solution for <em>T</em>(<em>n</em>).</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup> log <em>n</em>)"},{"key":"B","html":"Ο(n<sup>log2 3</sup> )"},{"key":"C","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>3</sup>)"},{"key":"D","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>)"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f76fc47ee9b4ae8585b7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A <em>B</em><sup>+</sup> tree index is built on the key ‘Sid’ attribute of the relation student. The attribute ‘Sid’ is of length 16 bytes, disk blocks are of size 512 bytes and index pointers are of size 6 bytes. What are the number of pointers per node?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"22"},{"key":"B","html":"23"},{"key":"C","html":"24"},{"key":"D","html":"26"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64ebb846378d158cf7df.png\" /></div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A Star Schema organizes data into a central fact table connected to multiple dimension tables,making it suitable for OLAP workloads."},{"key":"B","html":"Roll-Up is an OLAP operation that summarizes data by moving up a hierarchy or reducing the level of detail."},{"key":"C","html":"OLTP systems are optimized for transactional workloads rather than analytical queries."},{"key":"D","html":"In a Star Schema, dimension tables are always fully normalized to reduce redundancy."}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)<br />\n(a) A star schema uses one fact table linked to several denormalized dimension tables. The structure<br />\nsupports fast query performance and efficient aggregation for analytical tasks. Its simplicity<br />\nreduces join complexity, making it ideal for OLAP. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n(b) Roll-up aggregates values from a lower level to a higher level, such as days &rarr; months<br />\n&rarr; years. It helps in viewing summarized patterns and reduces the level of granularity. So,<br />\noption (b) is correct.<br />\n(c) OLTP systems handle frequent inserts, updates, and deletes with low latency. They ensure<br />\nconsistency and concurrency for business transactions. Thus, they are not intended for analytical<br />\nworkloads. So, option (c) is correct.<br />\n(d) Star schemas intentionally use denormalized dimension tables. Denormalization improves<br />\nperformance by reducing the number of joins needed in OLAP queries. Therefore, fully<br />\nnormalizing them would defeat the purpose of a star schema. So, option (d) is incorrect.</p>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":45,"MSQ":8,"NAT":12},"withoutKey":0}};
